BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:INTO THE LIGHT: Light-filled aintings by Katy Boggs
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260517T100000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260517T170000
UID:260501KHTzoRHot0C5X4gQiSJtIA
DESCRIPTION:Katy's paintings are an exploration of light—how it shapes f
 orm\, reveals color\, and defines what we see.\n\n"I am drawn to moments w
 here light is felt as much as seen: filtering through dense leaves\, tower
 ing in the crowns of trees\, glinting off cresting waves\, or softening in
 to the quiet glow of a sunset. Light is everywhere\; without it\, there is
  no seeing. Beyond its physical presence\, it also carries a deeper resona
 nce as a symbol of hope. In a time of uncertainty\, this work seeks to bri
 ng light into the image\, both visually and metaphorically."\n\nReception:
  April 25\, 4 to 6 pm.
LOCATION:Sebastopol Gallery
URL:http://sebastopolgallery.com
X-SOURCE:Press Democrat
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Sanctuary Concerts at UU Petaluma Presents: Baroque and {{Way}} Be
 yond
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260517T160000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260517T180000
UID:260430htgtq9QjaE6qcvBDjkHBdA
DESCRIPTION:On Sunday\, May 17 at 4 p.m.\, the Unitarian Universalists of 
 Petaluma’s Sanctuary Concert Series presents a classical concert entitle
 d “Baroque and {{Way}} Beyond.”\n\nThe series highlights the work of t
 he Sanctuary Coalition of Sonoma County\, defending our migrant neighbors 
 from deportation threats.\n\nIn a wide-ranging program that ranges from He
 nry Purcell onward through the centuries\, soprano Rachel Walters Steiner\
 , trumpeter Daniel Norris\, and pianist Yvonne Wormer will showcase a thei
 r combined talents in various configurations. \n\nNorris is a familiar si
 ght to audiences throughout the Bay Area\, where he is active as an orches
 tral musician (most often with Santa Rosa Symphony) and trumpet soloist. A
 s a recitalist and recording artist\, his special interests include the di
 scovery and promotion of new music for trumpet\, the rediscovery of rarely
 -performed older pieces\, and trumpet music composed by women. He has perf
 ormed and recorded the world premieres of many such works.\n\nAn accomplis
 hed multi-instrumentalist\, Norris has performed professionally on a broad
  assortment of instruments including trumpet\, French horn\, flute\, didge
 ridoo\, and ukulele. He can be heard playing several of these on the sound
 tracks to the video games *Sam and Max* and *Wallace and Gromit’s Grand 
 Adventures*.\n\nNorris is a graduate of California State University\, East
  Bay (B.A in Music\, 2001) and the Peabody Conservatory of Music (M.M. in 
 Trumpet Performance\, 2005) and is a certified Suzuki Method brass instruc
 tor. He is part of the music department faculty at Sonoma State University
  and Santa Rosa Junior College. \n\nSteiner is the Cantor and Choir Direc
 tor of the Unitarian Universalists of Petaluma\, where she regularly leads
  music and conducts the “Larking About” choir. A graduate of Sonoma St
 ate University with a degree in Music Education emphasizing Vocal Performa
 nce\, as well as a teaching credential in music\, Steiner is pursuing a Ma
 sters in Vocal Pedagogy. She has performed extensively in classical\, oper
 a\, and theater settings\, including a recent leading role in Purcell’s 
 “Dido and Aeneas.”\n\nPianist Wormer has been a collaborative pianist 
 ever since her uncle commented that “she phrases like a singer” when a
 t a family gathering early in her music studies. Though it took a few atte
 mpts\, she eventually graduated from Cal State Hayward (now East Bay)\, an
 d has gone on to accompany choirs\, singers\, and instrumentalists. She is
  particularly intuitive and this continues to be her strength. She loves t
 he collaborative aspect of accompanying\, and loves poetry and prose. The 
 combination of text and music\, and the wide variety of students and instr
 uctors makes her particularly well suited for her job as a staff accompani
 st at a university. She has worked intimately in the voice department with
  many many teachers and students over the past twenty-plus years. She is c
 urrently also the rehearsal pianist for the Sonoma Bach choir\, and the li
 turgical pianist at Congregation Shomrei Torah\, a progressive reform syna
 gogue in Sonoma county.\n\n“We’re excited to bring a new and beautiful
  listening room to the already rich Petaluma musical landscape\,” says D
 avid Dodd\, one of the series organizers. “The church sanctuary is an ac
 oustical gem\, and we are happy to offer this space for pure musical enjoy
 ment.” The building\, a 1901 Italian Gothic Revival gem by noted Petalum
 a architect Brainerd Jones\, is undergoing restoration by the congregation
 . Dodd says that the UU congregation sees these concerts as a way to raise
  awareness of the need for defense of our migrant neighbors in the face of
  ever-increasing threats of deportation. \n\nThis is an all-ages event\, 
 with no alcohol served–just some snacks and drinks for a late afternoon 
 concert. A suggested donation at the door–no advance ticket sales–of $
 20 (or more!) will go directly to the artists. The church is located at th
 e corner of 5th and B Streets in downtown Petaluma. Doors open at 3:30\, s
 o plan to arrive early to get a good seat!
LOCATION:Unitarian Universalists of Petaluma
URL:https://www.facebook.com/events/1698145118290998/
X-SOURCE:Press Democrat
END:VEVENT
